I don't want a great deal of money;
 My only care is not to be lonely;
 I'm glad there are some folks as happy as me
 With nothing but bread and butter for tea.

Then out came a huge man, as big as the moon;
As white was his skin, as bright were his eyes;
His long beard it hung down till it touched the ground,
Which he shook from his shoulders, as if it were a kite.

The knight came forward, and bowed himself low;
Then straight went on into another hall.
He had not time or leisure for an eye,
But only heard what he was told to do;
For now it seemed as though there were a crew
Riding from all directions towards the Hall.

The story of Achilles and Hector.

Achilles is an inveterate drunkard;
Hector, on the other hand, has no need for drink. In battle they fight side by side against the Trojans. The gods are watching them, who send one to each, so that neither may know who it is until afterwards when both men have fallen in the struggle. When they see their comrade lying dead at their feet, both fall into tears and lamentation. At last, Hector’s mother comes and takes him from where he lies, while Achilleus mourns his friend. Now all

The sun shines brightly on my window;
 The breeze is soft and sweet;
 I lean against the lattice-work there,
 And watch the morning fleet.
 My thoughts are on some one who is dear
 To me—and now she passes by;
 Her gentle footsteps make no sound as they tread
 On the leaves that gently sigh.

I am not happy or unhappy;
 Nor do I feel depressed;
 But simply, just because it is so good,
 I am content to rest. 

<strong>The above two verses are written by Rabindranath Tagore.

There are some things which cannot be said :
They speak themselves : they take no words at aid;
Their meaning rests upon a deeper plane —
The thing itself, and not the form assigned;

So when we meet in heaven, I shall be found
Not for my words, but only what I am.
